<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">steal</ansi>

Attempt to steal gold or items from an NPC, or take from
a container without being noticed. You cannot choose what
you steal -- you grab whatever you can get your hands on.

<ansi fg="yellow">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">steal <npc name></ansi>     Steal from an NPC
  <ansi fg="command">steal <container></ansi>    Steal from a container
  <ansi fg="command">steal <thing></ansi>        Take something that belongs to a household

<ansi fg="yellow">Examples:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">steal merchant</ansi>
  <ansi fg="command">steal chest</ansi>

Being hidden improves your chances. On failure, your cover
is blown and the target may attack. Cannot be used on
other players, or on anyone's companion, your own included.

Requires <ansi fg="skill">skullduggery</ansi> rank 2.

<ansi fg="yellow">Picking a pocket:</ansi>

Stealing from an NPC takes a moment. Your fingers are in the
pocket before you know whether you were seen, and quick hands
(<ansi fg="yellow">Dexterity</ansi>) make the moment shorter. Walk away or get into a
fight before it is done, and you lose your chance, but only if
your hands were quick enough: if the mark felt them, walking away
does not help, and they cry thief all the same. Now and then
a pocket holds a small curiosity of its own, worth a coin or two
to the right merchant, and often more than the odds and ends
people leave lying about.

<ansi fg="yellow">Things that belong to a household:</ansi>

Something you find while searching someone's home, with one of the
household about, is theirs, shown as, for example, "a small doll
<ansi fg="noun">(on the bookshelf)</ansi>". <ansi fg="command">get</ansi> will not take it, and neither will
<ansi fg="command">get all</ansi>, so you never steal by accident. To take it anyway,
<ansi fg="command">steal</ansi> it (<ansi fg="command">steal doll</ansi>). It is a theft like any other: your
<ansi fg="skill">skullduggery</ansi> is tested against the most watchful person in the
room, and being hidden helps. Seen by one of the household, you
are treated as a thief caught stealing from them; seen by anyone
else, your cover is blown. Either way the thing stays where it was.
What a household keeps tends to be worth more than what lies about
in the street, which is some comfort for the risk.

<ansi fg="yellow">A merchant's chest:</ansi>

Every merchant keeps a locked chest in the shop, and restocks it
with coin and a few of their goods from time to time. A merchant
with finer stock keeps a better lock and a sharper eye. Pick the
lock first (<ansi fg="command">help picklock</ansi>); <ansi fg="command">steal</ansi> will not get through a lock.
Setting your pick to it, and reaching in once it is open, are both
tested against the most watchful person in the room. A merchant
who sees you shouts thief, slams the chest shut and locks it, and
you are treated as caught stealing from them. A sleeping merchant
notices far less, and one who is out cannot notice at all. A picked
chest locks itself again before long, and when a merchant restocks,
whatever is left in the chest is cleared out and the lock is changed.

Whatever you take from a merchant's chest is stolen goods, marked
<ansi fg="item-stolen">s</ansi> in your pack, and it is hot for three days in the town you
took it in, just like a stolen trinket (see below). While it is hot
you cannot craft with it or salvage it. The merchant may recognise
it on you anywhere, even worn or in your hand; being recognised by
them is the same as being caught in the act. The town's guards and
watchmen may recognise it on you while you are still in that town,
and they report the theft to the law. Give it back to the merchant
and it is theirs again. A merchant's chest is not yours to fill:
you cannot put anything into one.

<ansi fg="yellow">After the theft:</ansi>

A stolen trinket, or anything from a merchant's chest, stays "hot"
for three days, in the town where you took it. There, no honest
merchant will buy it, no storage will hold it, and the auction house
will not list it; somewhere else it is ordinary goods. A fence will
buy it anywhere, and pays well for stolen goods. While it is hot,
the one you took it from may recognise it on you if your paths
cross, wherever that is, and that is the same as being caught in the
act. Give it back to its owner and it is no longer hot; but walk in
carrying it and they may spot it before you can hand it over, so go
quietly. A thief who has been caught stealing wins back a little of
the goodwill that cost with each trinket they return, though never as
much as the catch itself, and never more than they lost.

